-- title:
--   Theorem 4.6: the submonoid generated by $A$, $B$, $B^{-1}$ is the free product of $\langle A\rangle$ and $\langle B\rangle$
-- statement:
--   Let $A, B$ be the two generators of Thompson's group $F$ (the order isomorphisms of $[0,1]$
--   named `mapA`, `mapB` in the definition bundle). Consider the monoid homomorphism from the free
--   product (coproduct of monoids) of the free monoid on one generator with the infinite cyclic
--   group $\mathbb{Z}$,
--   $$\varphi\colon \mathbb{N} * \mathbb{Z} \longrightarrow \operatorname{Aut}[0,1],$$
--   sending the generator of $\mathbb{N}$ to $A$ and $1 \in \mathbb{Z}$ to $B$. The theorem
--   asserts that $\varphi$ is injective.
--
--   Equivalently: two words in the letters $A$, $B$, $B^{-1}$ that are reduced (no adjacent
--   $BB^{-1}$ or $B^{-1}B$) and distinct represent distinct elements of $F$, which is the source's
--   phrasing — the submonoid of $F$ generated by $A$, $B$, $B^{-1}$ is the free product of the
--   submonoid generated by $A$ and the subgroup generated by $B$. The statement lives in the
--   ambient group of order isomorphisms of $[0,1]$; that $A$ and $B$ lie in $F$ is Corollary 2.6
--   (milestone 5) and is not part of this statement.
-- source:
--   Cannon, J. W., Floyd, W. J., Parry, W. R., Introductory notes on Richard Thompson's groups, L'Enseignement Mathématique (2) 42 (1996) 215–256, https://doi.org/10.5169/seals-87877, Theorem 4.6, p. 230.

import Definitions.Def_CannonFloydParry
import Mathlib

namespace CannonFloydParry

/-- Theorem 4.6.  The monoid homomorphism from the free product of the free monoid on one
generator with the infinite cyclic group into `F`, sending the generator to `A` and `1 ∈ ℤ` to
`B`, is injective: the submonoid of `F` generated by `A`, `B`, `B⁻¹` is the free product of the
submonoid generated by `A` and the subgroup generated by `B`. -/
theorem coprodLift_mapA_mapB_injective :
    Function.Injective
      (Monoid.Coprod.lift (FreeMonoid.lift fun _ : Unit => mapA) (zpowersHom _ mapB)) := by
  sorry

end CannonFloydParry
